{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793581349,"nonce":"zgOgDXJpBPm5qC7sTBAhZxa3/Ng=","title":"Optimizer rounds spend time on edge rebuilds, dominator trees and use scans they repeat","message":"In a profile of Lua method_call start-up the optimizer is a quarter of all samples, and in it `rebuild_cfg_edges` (a hash map of vectors made per call) is 2.2%, `DominatorTree::new` 2.1% (the cache kept one tree, so a pass over a module's functions never found the previous pass's tree) and jump_thread's per-merge scan of the function 1%.\n\nNow: the edge rebuild fills the lists in place; LICM and the inliner use it, keeping their reading of a pattern match as a terminator with no successors; the dominator cache keeps eight trees; jump_thread indexes the reads of the merge phis once per round and checks dominance after threading only in debug builds (the merge defines nothing else, so a read elsewhere is refused before the rewrite).\n\nSuite A/B with the start-up change above, NUC, 5 interleaved reps of 22 kernels: geomean wall 0.989; richards -4%, scimark fft -5.6%, lu -4.8%, float -5.4%, deltablue -3.4%; long kernels within noise; CPU +1.4%.","files":null}]}